            0.447,
            0.434,
            0.684,
            0.45
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "n "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "change in temperature.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.413,
            0.457,
            0.9,
            0.539
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "As we observe the changes around us, we can see that there is a large variety of chemical reactions taking place around us. We will study about the various types of chemical reactions and their symbolic representation in this Chapter.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.279,
            0.567,
            0.599,
            0.588
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1.1 CHEMICAL EQUA TIONS"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 20,
          "bbox": [
            0.276,
            0.596,
            0.901,
            0.677
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Activity 1.1 can be described as – when a magnesium ribbon is burnt in oxygen, it gets converted to magnesium oxide. This description of a chemical reaction in a sentence form is quite long. It can be written in a shorter form. The simplest way to do this is to write it in the form of a word-equation.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 21,
          "bbox": [
            0.278,
            0.677,
            0.747,
            0.694
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The word-equation for the above reaction would be –",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 22,
          "bbox": [
            0.33,
            0.7,
            0.897,
            0.733
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_1_equation_22.jpg",
          "content": "\\begin{array} { c c c } { { \\mathrm { M a g n e s i u m } + \\mathrm { O x y g e n } } } & { { \\rightarrow } } & { { \\mathrm { M a g n e s i u m ~ o x i d e } } } \\\\ { { \\mathrm { ( R e a c t a n t s ) } } } & { { } } & { { \\mathrm { ( P r o d u c t ) } } } \\end{array}\\tag{1.1}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 23,
          "bbox": [
            0.277,
            0.74,
            0.899,
            0.789
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The substances that undergo chemical change in the reaction (1.1), magnesium and oxygen, are the reactants. The new substance is magnesium oxide, formed during the reaction, as a product.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 24,
          "bbox": [
            0.276,
            0.789,
            0.901,
            0.886
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "A word-equation shows change of reactants to products through an arrow placed between them. The reactants are written on the left-hand side (LHS) with a plus sign (+) between them. Similarly, products are written on the right-hand side (RHS) with a plus sign (+) between them. The arrowhead points towards the products, and shows the direction of the reaction.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
